WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.633
Consider the two instructions.

2
00:00:02.633 --> 00:00:09.040
The load reads a 32-bit word from the address in x10, producing a new x5.

3
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.428
The add combines that value with x7 to produce x6.

4
00:00:13.428 --> 00:00:18.869
Assume a valid word-aligned address, a memory value of twelve,

5
00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:23.784
x7 ready with value three, and no exceptions or flushes.

6
00:00:23.784 --> 00:00:28.787
Decode can identify register numbers before data returns,

7
00:00:28.787 --> 00:00:32.912
but cannot supply a result that does not exist.

8
00:00:32.912 --> 00:00:39.055
The add needs the new x5 from the load: a read-after-write dependency.

9
00:00:39.055 --> 00:00:45.374
A free integer unit cannot replace that missing operand with the old x5.

10
00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:53.010
In this RV32 single-issue baseline, the scoreboard tracks readiness and can forward the

11
00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.414
returned twelve.

12
00:00:54.414 --> 00:01:00.821
The x5 equals twelve label on the ALU input denotes an available operand,

13
00:01:00.821 --> 00:01:06.262
not proof that the architectural register has already changed.

14
00:01:06.262 --> 00:01:11.177
With the conditions satisfied, the add produces fifteen.

15
00:01:11.177 --> 00:01:15.917
Both completed instructions still pass through commit;

16
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:23.552
after normal retirement in program order, architectural x5 is twelve and x6 is fifteen.

17
00:01:23.552 --> 00:01:28.379
The dashed load path does not make retirement optional.

18
00:01:28.379 --> 00:01:35.400
These are conceptual events, not fixed cycles or a guaranteed forwarding window.

19
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.648
Cache and bus details are simplified.

20
00:01:38.648 --> 00:01:46.020
Advance the states to separate result availability from architectural-state updates.

